    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 10 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 10. Navigating the special education system in New York City can be challenging. This directory provides key contact information and actionable advice to help you advocate effectively for your child's needs.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 1 (Serving District 10)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>1 Fordham Plaza, Bronx, NY 10458</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-329-8001">718-329-8001</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
                <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #777; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Note:</strong> Utilizing your NYC Schools Account (NYCSA) for email communication ensures a direct line to the CSE.  If you don't have an account, create one to track communication and access student records.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Persistence is key. Make sure to keep meticulous records of all communication attempts.  See the communication protocol below for more detailed instructions.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window outlined in 8 NYCRR §200.5(a)(1)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is crucial for initiating evaluations, requesting IEP meetings, or filing complaints.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary, reiterating what was discussed and any agreements made.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> for initial referrals, or <em>"IEP Meeting Request - [Student Name]"</em> for meetings. This helps ensure your email is properly categorized.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates, names, methods of communication (phone, email), and a brief summary of the conversation. This log is invaluable if you need to file a complaint.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call to the CSE contact.  Clearly state your request and the reason for contacting them.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).  Politely reiterate your request and mention that you are copying the school principal for awareness. Example: "Dear [CSE Contact], Following up on my email from [Date] regarding [Student Name]'s [IEP/Evaluation]. I have copied Principal [Principal's Name] for their awareness. Thank you."</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.  This is a formal escalation. Be direct and concise, outlining the previous attempts to contact the CSE and the lack of response.  Include specific dates and a summary of your initial request.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or if the district fails to respond to your concerns.  This is a formal complaint process with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) and can trigger an investigation. Instructions on filing a State Complaint can be found on the NYSED website. (See helpful links below).</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What is the Committee on Special Education (CSE)?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">The CSE is a multidisciplinary team responsible for evaluating students suspected of having a disability and developing Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) for eligible students. The CSE is mandated by the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Education Law. See 8 NYCRR §200.3 for specific regulations. The CSE team typically includes the parent(s), a special education teacher, a general education teacher, a school psychologist, and a district representative.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What is the timeline for a special education evaluation in NYC?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">According to New York State regulations (8 NYCRR §200.4(a)), the district must complete the initial evaluation within 60 school days of receiving parental consent. This timeline can be challenging to adhere to in NYC, so it is crucial to track the dates and communicate proactively with the CSE.</p>
    </div>

     <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">My child's IEP isn't being followed. What should I do?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">First, document every instance where the IEP is not being implemented. This includes dates, times, specific services missed, and any staff involved. Then, schedule an IEP meeting with the CSE to discuss your concerns. If the issues persist, you can consider mediation, impartial hearings, or filing a State Complaint with NYSED. Remember to keep all communication in writing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What if I disagree with the CSE's recommendations?</h3>
         <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">You have the right to disagree with the CSE's recommendations. You can request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you believe the school's evaluation was inadequate. You also have the right to mediation or an impartial hearing to resolve disputes. IDEA provides parents with these procedural safeguards to ensure their voices are heard (34 CFR §300.507).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What are some common pitfalls parents encounter in NYC District 10?</h3>
        <ul style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li><strong>Delays in evaluations:</strong> Be proactive in following up with the CSE to ensure evaluations are completed within the mandated timelines.</li>
            <li><strong>Inadequate IEP goals:</strong> Ensure that IEP goals are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART goals).</li>
            <li><strong>Lack of related services:</strong> Advocate for appropriate related services, such as speech therapy, occupational therapy, or counseling, as outlined in the IEP.</li>
            <li><strong>Difficulty scheduling IEP meetings:</strong> Be persistent in scheduling IEP meetings at a time that is convenient for you.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="helpful-links" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-link"></i> Helpful Links
    </h2>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="http://www.nysed.gov/specialed/publications/policy-memos" target="_blank">NYSED Policy Memos and Guidance</a>: Access official policy memos and guidance documents from the New York State Education Department.</li>
        <li><a href="http://www.p12.nysed.gov/specialed/quality/qssrs/policyguide/" target="_blank">Quality Indicators Review and Resource Suite (QIRRS)</a>: Learn about the quality indicators used to evaluate special education programs in New York State.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.parentcenterhub.org/" target="_blank">Parent Center Hub</a>: Find resources and support for parents of children with disabilities.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.understood.org/" target="_blank">Understood.org</a>: A comprehensive website with information and resources on learning disabilities and ADHD.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.nyc.gov/site/d75/about/about.page" target="_blank">NYC Department of Education - District 75</a>: Information about District 75, which provides specialized programs for students with significant disabilities.</li>
         <li><a href="https://www.nysed.gov/specialed/how-file-complaint" target="_blank">How to File a Complaint with NYSED</a>: Direct link to NYSED's complaint process.</li>
    </ul>
</section>


